WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:01.950
<v Instructor>So a small service that can come up</v>

2
00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:04.920
in the exam is a billing service called Cost Explorer,

3
00:00:04.920 --> 00:00:06.870
which is used to visualize, understand,

4
00:00:06.870 --> 00:00:09.960
and manage your AWS cost and usage over time.

5
00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:11.550
It allows you to create custom reports

6
00:00:11.550 --> 00:00:14.370
that analyze your cost and usage data.

7
00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:15.840
And so with this, you get dashboards,

8
00:00:15.840 --> 00:00:17.820
and diagrams, and so on.

9
00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:19.860
And so you can analyze your data at a high level,

10
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:22.590
for example, total cost and usage across all accounts,

11
00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.800
but you can also go down to get it monthly, hourly,

12
00:00:25.800 --> 00:00:28.020
or at the resource level granularity.

13
00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:29.067
And then thanks to Cost Explorer,

14
00:00:29.067 --> 00:00:30.780
you can do some cost savings

15
00:00:30.780 --> 00:00:34.110
because it allows you to choose an optimal savings plan

16
00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:36.360
to lower the prices on your bill.

17
00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:37.830
You can forecast usage as well

18
00:00:37.830 --> 00:00:40.620
up to 18 months based on previous usage,

19
00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.840
which can be helpful for cost planning.

20
00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:45.570
So if we look at Cost Explorer, here's an example.

21
00:00:45.570 --> 00:00:48.420
We get monthly cost by AWS service.

22
00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.310
In this example, we can see, for example,

23
00:00:50.310 --> 00:00:53.850
that some instances based on instance type

24
00:00:53.850 --> 00:00:55.320
can be more expensive than others,

25
00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:57.210
and so we can start optimizing our costs,

26
00:00:57.210 --> 00:00:58.537
asking ourselves the question,

27
00:00:58.537 --> 00:01:00.990
"Are these instances correctly used?

28
00:01:00.990 --> 00:01:02.160
Are they maximally used?

29
00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:03.120
Are they the right size?"

30
00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:04.650
These kind of things.

31
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:07.560
Then we can get also hourly and resource level.

32
00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.270
So in this example, we get to, again,

33
00:01:09.270 --> 00:01:12.300
some EC2 instance resource level information,

34
00:01:12.300 --> 00:01:15.810
and we get the cost over time, so we can see what happens

35
00:01:15.810 --> 00:01:18.330
every single hour to get a better breakdown

36
00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:20.190
and understanding of our bill.

37
00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:23.160
This is also to find, as I said, a savings plan.

38
00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:25.710
So savings plan is an alternative to reserved instances,

39
00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:26.850
we have seen this.

40
00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.680
And so thanks to Cost Explorer,

41
00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:33.300
we get this little popup that will come up

42
00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:36.030
and it will tell us what kind of savings plan

43
00:01:36.030 --> 00:01:38.460
we can have based on our usage,

44
00:01:38.460 --> 00:01:40.470
and it will give us some recommendation,

45
00:01:40.470 --> 00:01:43.230
and give us some estimated monthly spend, and so on.

46
00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:44.580
So this is quite handy.

47
00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:46.020
Finally, we can forecast usage.

48
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:50.220
So based on previous costs that we had in the past,

49
00:01:50.220 --> 00:01:52.950
we can get some forecasts and some confidence

50
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.410
of the forecast around how much we're expected to pay

51
00:01:55.410 --> 00:01:58.260
for our bill based on our previous usage,

52
00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:59.700
and that could be quite helpful.

53
00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.310
So these kind of use cases can come up in the exam,

54
00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:03.960
and this is probably the only billing service

55
00:02:03.960 --> 00:02:06.270
that AWS will ask you in the exam.

56
00:02:06.270 --> 00:02:08.720
So that's it, I will see you in the next lecture.

